
  # CAP theorem
  CAP = Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance.

  The CAP theorem says that when a network partition occurs,
  a distributed system cannot simultaneously guarantee both Consistency and Availability.

  Consistency (C): Every read gets the latest successful write (or an error).
  Availability (A): Every request to a non-failing node gets a response, though it may not contain the latest data.
  Partition Tolerance (P): The system continues operating despite communication failures between parts of the network.

  Simple example

  Imagine a database replicated across Server A and Server B. If the network connection between them breaks:

  CP system: Prioritizes consistency. A server may reject requests rather than return potentially outdated data.

  AP system: Prioritizes availability. Both servers keep accepting/responding to requests, but their data can
  temporarily disagree.

  So the practical CAP choice during a partition is:

  CP → Consistency over Availability
  AP → Availability over Consistency

  A common misconception is describing CAP as simply “choose any 2 out of 3.” More precisely,
  when a partition exists, you must trade off consistency against availability.

 Common interview question
 Can a distributed system provide Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance simultaneously?
 Answer:
 No. During a network partition, you must choose between:

 CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance), or
 AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance).

 Since network partitions are unavoidable in distributed systems, modern distributed systems are generally designed
 as either CP or AP, depending on the application's requirements.

 Here's a simple decision guide:

 Requirement	                          Choose	Why
 Data correctness is critical	            CP	    Never return stale or conflicting data.
 Service must always remain available	    AP	    Continue serving requests even if data is temporarily inconsistent.

  Choose CP (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)
  Use CP when incorrect data is worse than temporary downtime.

  Examples
  🏦 Banking systems
  Account balance
  Money transfer
  💳 Payment gateways
  📈 Stock trading
  ✈️ Flight seat booking
  🎟️ Ticket reservation
  Inventory management (avoid overselling)
  Scenario

  Two users try to buy the last concert ticket simultaneously.

  A CP system allows only one purchase.
  The other user receives an error or waits.

  Correctness is maintained.

  Choose AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance)

  Use AP when the application should keep running even if data is temporarily out of sync.

  Examples
  👍 Social media likes
  💬 Chat applications
  📝 Comments
  News feeds
  DNS
  Product catalogs
  Video streaming recommendations
  Scenario

  You like a friend's post.

  Your friend may not see the new like for a few seconds, but the app never goes down.

  This is acceptable.

  Mnemonic
  CP = Correctness first
  Better to reject a request than return incorrect data.
  AP = Always available
  Better to respond immediately, even if the data is temporarily stale.



